{"reference":"Acts 10","book":"Acts","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Following the Vision’s Meaning","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The command to kill and eat is not left as a self-contained instruction: Peter remains perplexed about its meaning, then goes with Cornelius’s messengers, enters a Gentile household, and says that God has shown him not to call any person common or unclean. The account then centers on hospitality, Peter’s message, and the Spirit’s reception by Gentiles. This narrative movement cautions against treating the vision as a simple, timeless rule about diet apart from its role in the chapter.","verse_refs":["Acts 10:1-48","Acts 10:24-48","Acts 10:9-20"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_010_a156ba491d03be9ab9a99c60"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Household Baptism","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Peter orders baptism after the Spirit comes upon those gathered in Cornelius’s household. Acts narrates household baptisms, but it does not specify the age or belief status of every member of every household. For that reason, arguments about infant baptism cannot be settled from this episode alone and remain dependent on wider tradition and interpretation.","verse_refs":["Acts 10:44-48"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_010_9fa8bd5671510079d73bfc58"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts Historically","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Questions about Acts’ historical reliability are best considered claim by claim, comparing particular details with independent evidence where such evidence is available, rather than assigning the whole book a blanket verdict of either reliability or unreliability.","confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact","provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_010_d71c95f5860c81d318fc6c1f"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}